{Layer_Schema} :=

LAYER(
  id          := [generated layer id],
  name        := [unique layer name: sources | medium_type | workflow_name | property_name | other canonical layer name],
  metaform_id := [metaform id],
  description := [briefly describe this layer],

  elements := [
    ELEMENT(
      namespace_address := [dotted namespace address],
      role              := [AUE|CQU|DE|SE|NE],

      refs := [
        REF(
          key   := [key],
          value := [value]
        )
      ],

      elements := [
        ELEMENT(
          namespace_address := [dotted namespace address],
          role              := [DE|SE|NE],
          refs              := [],
          joins             := []
        )
      ],

      joins := [
        JOIN(
          type     := purpose,
          purpose  := [RE Operative Purpose],
          ef       := [PE Encounter Function within this RE encounter],
          ifd      := [send|receive|both],
          elements := []
        )
      ]
    )
  ]
)

# Notes
+> name identifies the knowledge or function contributed by the layer.
+> description states what the layer holds and how its values are read, so an agent can use it without inferring from the name.
+> Layer names are unique across all layers of the metaform.
+> A layer name can be used as a namespace root, so it is one valid namespace segment: lowercase letters, digits and underscores; no spaces, dots or hyphens.
+> A layer name is never a project root: code, document, media, data, upstream or downstream.
+> Use stable canonical names such as sources, medium_type, workflow_name, or property_name.
+> A layer overlays the metaform identified by metaform_id using the same namespace structure.
+> The layer may contain only the portions of the metaform for which it contributes knowledge.
+> An element is named by its namespace_address alone; its name and name_length come from the metaform.
